    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nExpanding beyond existing studies of hospitals’ role in narratives of survival post-assault, this essay focuses on the distinctive experience of African American survivors of sexual assault today, and considers the historical contexts that inform their medical care. The author shows the disparities in care of African American survivors of assault, noting that the disparities extend to obstetrics, and argues for remedies for these disparities, as justice for survivors begins\r\nin the clinic.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
